## 15. VPS DEPLOYMENT READINESS

Prepare the existing application for a controlled production deployment on the planned VPS, approximately 4 CPU cores and 8 GB RAM.

Avoid unnecessary infrastructure, heavyweight local AI models, or multiple services that do not justify their cost.

Verify or document:

- Production build and startup commands.
- Runtime and package-manager requirements.
- Required environment variables.
- Secure secret injection.
- Database setup and migration sequence.
- Persistent storage requirements.
- Reverse proxy and HTTPS configuration.
- Process supervision and restart behavior.
- Background job startup and shutdown.
- Health and readiness endpoints.
- Log rotation and storage limits.
- Database and application backup procedures.
- A practical restoration procedure.
- Deployment and rollback steps.
- Graceful shutdown and recovery after restart.

Never overwrite production data or run destructive migrations without a deliberate, documented migration strategy.

Provide a production environment-variable template with descriptions and safe placeholders. Never put real secrets into the repository, generated documentation, or client-side code.

If deployment credentials or access to the VPS are not available, prepare and test the deployment configuration locally to the extent possible, then provide explicit manual deployment steps. Do not claim that the application has been deployed.

## 16. OBSERVABILITY AND OPERATIONAL READINESS

Make production failures diagnosable.

Provide structured, useful server-side logging without exposing passwords, access tokens, API keys, sensitive personal information, or complete secret-bearing request URLs.

Where practical, expose health information that distinguishes:

- Application process health.
- Database connectivity.
- Background job status.
- Provider connectivity.
- Last successful ingestion.
- Data freshness.
- Configuration problems.

Do not expose sensitive internal diagnostic details to unauthenticated visitors.

Do not report a provider as healthy merely because its configuration exists. Health status must reflect actual checks and their timestamps.

Keep logs and monitoring within the available VPS resource budget.

